[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Creating a node still costs a reload each, where every other write batches

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[WORK]
UID: WORK-SCRIBE-NEW-DOES-NOT-BATCH
TITLE: Creating a node still costs a reload each, where every other write batches
DEPTH: needs-design
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Every other write defers its reload, so a run of them pays one. `new` does
not: it reloads inside the operation, because the @repo grammar alias is
resolved by the index builder and not by the reader. Measured: three creates
cost 2462 ms, about 820 ms each, against 17 ms for three sets.

THE FACT THAT MAKES THIS TRACTABLE, measured on the live canon: 382
documents hold 382 distinct DocumentGrammar objects but exactly ONE shared
element list. The parsed element definitions the alias resolves to are a
single object already in memory that never changes. The reload re-reads 382
files to obtain a reference to something that never moved.

So the shape is: construct a DocumentGrammar around the existing shared
element list, derive the new document's DocumentMeta, and insert into
document_list, map_docs_by_paths and map_docs_by_rel_paths. No file re-read,
no re-resolution, and `new` becomes an ordinary deferred write that batches
like the others.

The care is in the meta, not the grammar. Of six directory-scoped fields
compared between two documents in one directory, three matched and three did
not, so the rest must be derived from the path -- which means hand-mirroring
document_finder, the thing the workspace otherwise refuses to do. That is
made safe by a contract rather than by care: adopt, then full-reload, then
compare EVERY meta field. A prior probe did this round trip and got 10 of 10
fields identical to a genuine rebuild at about 25 ms.
<<<
